 ## Overview
-This method projects a set of 3D Gaussians onto a 2D image plane to generate a rendered image, solving the problem of efficiently rasterizing volumetric scene representations without explicit ray-marching. It differs from sibling methods like [Ray-marching on 3D Gaussians](1036-ray-marching-on-3d-gaussians.md) by using explicit screen-space splatting with alpha compositing, and from [Row-constant hoisting for Gaussian Splatting Rasterization](1195-row-constant-hoisting-for-gaussian-splatting-rasterization.md) by defining the core projection and covariance stabilization logic rather than just the inner-loop optimization. The key distinction is the linearization of the perspective projection via the Jacobian to approximate the resulting 2D Gaussian, combined with a diagonal epsilon stabilization to ensure numerical invertibility.
+This method projects a set of 3D Gaussians onto a 2D image plane to generate a rendered image, solving the problem of efficiently rasterizing volumetric scene representations without explicit ray-marching [S2, unofficial]. It differs from sibling methods like [Ray-marching on 3D Gaussians](1036-ray-marching-on-3d-gaussians.md) by using explicit screen-space splatting with alpha compositing, and from [Row-constant hoisting for Gaussian Splatting Rasterization](1195-row-constant-hoisting-for-gaussian-splatting-rasterization.md) by defining the core projection and covariance stabilization logic rather than just the inner-loop optimization [G]. The key distinction is the linearization of the perspective projection via the Jacobian to approximate the resulting 2D Gaussian, combined with a diagonal epsilon stabilization to ensure numerical invertibility [S1, unofficial].
 
